val.\n\n- The interval $[\\frac{1}{2},1]$   is closed, and the function is continuous on $[\\frac{1}{2},1].$   Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $[0,\\frac{1}{4})$  is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[0,1]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on it because it is not defined at $x=0.$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[\\frac{1}{2},1]$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341988 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175903 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bbc6a2d-2078-50d5-9584-c705c19212da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals on Which a Given Function Satisfies the Extreme Value Theorem" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048748 :topic/id #uuid "bad1cfe2-7639-5714-8417-2b8af7ece5cb" :topic/math-academy-id 3571 :topic/title "The Extreme Value Theorem"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7bd74156-928d-5b74-aebc-4099a1b5eafe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468034} {:answer/id #uuid "2c06241b-b19c-5161-aaf1-8c7f6a5ba49a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468035}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bd74156-928d-5b74-aebc-4099a1b5eafe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468034} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a215495-71de-5c81-81e9-7523fa7d2d25" :answer-field/key "$[0,1]$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468738} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e95fe8db-c741-525c-8be8-9578b9cc090a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468036