=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0da5999b-91a9-5965-95f6-10d07219e7e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-79826" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=x\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a function and the known \nsolutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$ only."}]
